{
  "example": "A definition named `glossary.first_parent_history` declares the phrase \"first-parent history\". A term reference to that name renders the phrase as an ordinary hyperlink to the declaration, without displaying the identifier or a badge. {cite}`requirements.definitions.references`",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** The example has a test and the implementation does what the example describes. The test does not assert one of the example's stated outcomes, so I judge it not fully covered.\n\n**What covers the example.** The only `{test}` reference to `requirements.definitions.phrase_rendering` is `phrases_are_plain_links_and_markdown_literals_are_preserved` (tests/definitions.rs:291-326).\n- It declares a definition `glossary.a` with the phrase `*alpha* & <beta>`, then references it from a nested chapter with `{term}`.\n- It asserts that the declaration is anchored as `habeas-definition-glossary.a` and that `:phrase:` is not leaked.\n- It asserts that `shields.io` does not appear, so no badge is rendered.\n- It asserts exactly one `href=\"../glossary.html#habeas-definition-glossary.a\"`, so the reference is a link to the anchored declaration. The escaped, backticked and fenced forms are left literal.\n- It asserts that the escaped phrase `&#42;alpha&#42; &amp; &lt;beta&gt;` appears.\n\n**Implementation, for context.** `replace_references` in src/lib.rs (384-442) renders a Term reference as `<a href=\"…#anchor\">{escaped_phrase}</a>`. It adds no badge or class, and the link text is only the phrase, via `terms::escaped_phrase` in src/terms.rs (187-200). So the behaviour matches the example.\n\n**The gap.**\n- The example says the phrase is rendered \"without displaying the identifier or a badge.\"\n- The test checks only for the absence of `shields.io`. It never checks that the link text is exactly the phrase, or that the identifier is not shown.\n- The chapter content contains `glossary.a` in the literal examples and in the `href`, so a simple absence check would not work. The test could instead assert the exact `<a href=\"…\">…</a>` string.\n- As written, a regression that rendered `glossary.a: alpha` as the link text, or a non-shields text badge, would still pass every assertion.\n- The \"ordinary hyperlink\" property, meaning no extra class or markup, is not asserted either.\n\nThe scenario is partly covered, but an explicit expected outcome has no assertion. The source contains no FIXME or other acknowledged gap, so the judgment is false rather than FIXME.",
  "inputs": [
    {
